{%- comment -%}
  image-fallback.liquid — the three-layer image path (build spec §6).

  No section in this theme may render a bare <img src>. Every media slot goes
  through here, so a store with no products and no uploads still renders a
  laid-out page rather than a broken one.

  Resolution order:
    1. `image` is set                          → the merchant's own image
    2. `fallback` set and settings.demo_images → a demo asset, if present
    3. neither                                 → placeholder_svg_tag

  **The theme ships no photography**, so layer 2 is inert on a stock install
  and every unset slot resolves to layer 3. That is deliberate: no stock
  imagery in the ZIP means no third-party licence travelling with the buyer's
  store and nothing for them to strip out. The layer stays because it costs
  nothing and it activates for anyone who does put matching files in assets/.

  The demo store reproduces the screenshots through layer 1 instead — the
  buyer uploads demo-store-export/media/ to their own Files and the shipped
  JSON resolves it by filename.

  Because layer 2 is normally absent, layer 3 is the common path rather than
  the emergency one, which is why the capture below matters: it must never
  leave a Liquid error where an image should be.

  Params:
    image         (image, optional)   — merchant image object
    fallback      (string, optional)  — demo asset filename, e.g. 'demo-hero.webp'
    alt           (string, optional)  — alt text; empty string for decorative
    widths        (string, optional)  — comma-separated srcset widths
    sizes         (string, optional)  — sizes attribute
    class         (string, optional)  — classes on the <img>
    loading       (string, optional)  — 'lazy' (default) or 'eager'
    fetchpriority (string, optional)  — 'high' on the LCP image only
    width         (number, optional)  — intrinsic width, required for layer 2
    height        (number, optional)  — intrinsic height, required for layer 2
    placeholder   (string, optional)  — placeholder_svg_tag name for layer 3

  Usage:
    {% render 'image-fallback',
      image: section.settings.image,
      fallback: 'demo-hero.webp',
      alt: section.settings.heading,
      width: 2400,
      height: 1350,
      sizes: '100vw',
      loading: 'eager',
      fetchpriority: 'high'
    %}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign image_widths = widths | default: '360,540,720,900,1080,1296,1512,1800,1920,2100,2400'
  assign image_sizes = sizes | default: '100vw'
  assign image_loading = loading | default: 'lazy'
  assign image_priority = fetchpriority | default: 'auto'
  assign image_alt = alt | default: '' | escape
  assign placeholder_name = placeholder | default: 'image'

  comment
    Every argument passed to image_tag below is given a concrete value.
    Passing a nil named argument renders an empty attribute
    (fetchpriority="") rather than omitting it, which is invalid for
    enumerated attributes — so defaults are resolved here, not inline.
  endcomment
  assign img_class = 'image-fallback__img'
  if class != blank
    assign img_class = img_class | append: ' ' | append: class
  endif

  assign use_fallback = false
  if image == blank and fallback != blank and settings.demo_images
    assign use_fallback = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if image != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Layer 1. width/height are deliberately not passed: image_tag derives
    both from the image object, which is more reliable than a caller's
    guess and is what reserves the space that keeps CLS at zero.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {{
    image
    | image_url: width: 2400
    | image_tag:
      widths: image_widths,
      sizes: image_sizes,
      alt: image_alt,
      class: img_class,
      loading: image_loading,
      fetchpriority: image_priority
  }}

{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Layer 2, captured rather than printed.

    `asset_url` happily returns a URL for a file that is not in the theme,
    and `image_url` then rejects it with "invalid url input" — a runtime
    Liquid error that neither theme-check nor the upload compiler catches,
    and which renders the error text straight into the page. That is worse
    than no image at all.

    So the attempt is captured and only used if it actually produced an
    <img>. Anything else — missing asset, unsupported file, a future filter
    change — falls through to layer 3. This is what makes the three-layer
    fallback in build spec §6 genuinely safe rather than merely ordered.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign rendered_fallback = '' -%}

  {%- if use_fallback -%}
    {%- capture rendered_fallback -%}
      {%- if width and height -%}
        {{-
          fallback
          | asset_url
          | image_url: width: 2400
          | image_tag:
            widths: image_widths,
            sizes: image_sizes,
            alt: image_alt,
            class: img_class,
            loading: image_loading,
            fetchpriority: image_priority,
            width: width,
            height: height
        -}}
      {%- else -%}
        {{-
          fallback
          | asset_url
          | image_url: width: 2400
          | image_tag:
            widths: image_widths,
            sizes: image_sizes,
            alt: image_alt,
            class: img_class,
            loading: image_loading,
            fetchpriority: image_priority
        -}}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if rendered_fallback contains '<img' -%}
    {{ rendered_fallback }}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%} Layer 3. {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ placeholder_name | placeholder_svg_tag: img_class }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
